                <pb facs="0860" n="848" sameAs="frus:frus1942v01/pg848"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00115A Pacific War/52: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Switzerland</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Harrison</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary
                        of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Bern</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian" when="1942-03-26"
                            >March 26, 1942</date>.<lb/>[Received March 26—1:33 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1231. American interests—Philippines. Department’s 446, February 14, last
                    paragraph. International Red Cross Committee replies to Legation’s request for
                    investigation Japanese treatment American civilians Philippines stating that
                    grave question as well as general problems [of] treatment civilians occupied
                    territory Far East having <pb facs="0861" n="849" xml:id="pg849"/> Committee’s
                    full attention. Committee has taken steps endeavor obtain information from
                    Japanese authorities regarding American civilians in Philippines and also asked
                    Japanese Government to agree appointment Committee delegate at Manila.</p>
                <p>Japanese reply not yet received but Committee pursuing endeavors obtain
                    information desired by American Government and will advise Legation when
                    received.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Harrison</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
